  mean:'Nurses do most of the "first touches" of a visit: the scale, the questions, the bandage. A child who knows the nurse arrives expecting the friendliest part of the day.',
  know:'You need the card and the teddy hospital props: toy thermometer, toy bandages. The nurse card sits beside the doctor card — one team, two sets of gentle hands.',
  why:'Nurses are the pattern for all kindness-with-competence: firm hands, soft voice, everything explained. Naming the nurse gives your child a person to look for — "where is the nurse?" is a much better question than a worry.',
  dev:'Helper vocabulary, care-giving pretend play (nursing is empathy with props), and question-answering with a safe grown-up nearby.',
  vocab:'nurse, care, measure, bandage, kind, team',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "a nurse helps care for you."\n2) List what nurses do: weigh you, measure how tall, ask how you feel, wrap a bandage.\n3) Open the teddy hospital: the child is the nurse, teddy is the patient.\n4) The nurse asks teddy: "how do you feel today?" — and waits for the answer.\n5) Swap roles: now the grown-up nurses, and the child says how THEY feel — the real skill, practiced.',
  say:'"Nurse! A nurse helps care for you. She weighs you, measures you, asks how you feel — and she knows all about bandages. She and the doctor are a team."',
